    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The core of the book is the Zhou yi, a divination manual of the Western Zhou, which modern scholars date to between the 10th and 4th centuries BCE.</li><li>Over the Warring States and early imperial periods it gained the Ten Wings and became a cosmological text; in 136 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a> named it the first among the classics.</li><li>Commentators of many schools have read it for divination, moral guidance and philosophy, and it has been translated into Western languages dozens of times.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuo-zhuan">Zuo Zhuan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guoyu">Guoyu</a> describe more than twenty divinations with the Zhou yi, made by professional soothsayers for royal families between 671 and 487 BCE, yet none of the stories uses a commentary or a stated method.</p></aside>
    </section>

