    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">last quarter of the 9th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Edward Shaughnessy dates the compilation of the Zhou yi in its current form to the early reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-xuan-of-zhou">King Xuan of Zhou</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">671 to 487 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuo-zhuan">Zuo Zhuan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guoyu">Guoyu</a> record more than twenty divinations using the Zhou yi for royal families</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 300 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Great Commentary, arguably the most important of the Ten Wings, is composed, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> Museum copy shows the Zhou yi in use at all levels of society</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">136 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a> names the Zhou yi the first among the classics and calls it the I Ching</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">3rd century</span><span class="rf-text">Wang Bi writes his Annotated Book of Changes, which becomes the official commentary under the Tang</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">12th century</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> reconstructs yarrow stalk divination from the Great Commentary, a method still in use</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1703</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Leibniz</a> writes the first European commentary on the I Ching</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1923</span><span class="rf-text">Richard Wilhelm's German translation appears; Cary Baynes's English version follows in 1950</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou.jpg" alt="Duke of Zhou" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duke_of_Zhou.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The core of the I Ching is a divination text of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a> called the Changes of Zhou (Zhou yi). Modern scholars suggest dates between the 10th and 4th centuries BCE for the assembly of the text in roughly its present form. The American sinologist Edward Shaughnessy, comparing its language with dated bronze inscriptions, dated its compilation to the last quarter of the 9th century BCE, in the early decades of the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-xuan-of-zhou">King Xuan of Zhou</a> (r. c. 827 to 782 BCE).</p><p>Tradition ascribed the work to the Zhou cultural heroes King Wen and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a>, and associated it with the legendary ruler Fuxi. Another tradition held that most of it was written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-of-shang">Tang of Shang</a>. According to the Great Commentary, Fuxi observed the patterns of the world and made the eight trigrams. The Zhou yi itself says nothing about its origins. By the Han, a consensus formed around Ma Rong's attribution to Fuxi, King Wen, the Duke of Zhou and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>, but scholars no longer generally accept it.</p><p>It may not have stood alone: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rites-of-zhou">Rites of Zhou</a> names two other divination systems, the Lianshan and the Guicang.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/king-wen-of-zhou"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/king-wen-of-zhou.jpg" alt="King Wen of Zhou" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/king-wen-of-zhou">King Wen of Zhou</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Wen_of_Zhou_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The basic unit of the Zhou yi is the hexagram (gua), a figure of six stacked lines, each either broken or unbroken. The received text holds all 64 possible hexagrams. Each has a name, a short hexagram statement and six line statements. Hexagrams 1 and 2 have an extra line statement. The book opens with the words yuan heng li zhen, which recur in the hexagram statements and were already treated as important in the 6th century BCE. Shaughnessy reads them as an "initial receipt" of an offering, "beneficial" for further "divining".</p><p>Yin and yang are represented by broken and solid lines. Three lines make one of eight trigrams, and two trigrams combined make a hexagram. The line statements, which form most of the book, are exceedingly cryptic. They may give oracular or prognostic statements, and some contain poetry or references to historical events.</p><p>In the canonical text the hexagrams follow the King Wen sequence, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wen-of-zhou">King Wen of Zhou</a>, who traditionally reformed the method of interpretation. The sequence generally pairs each hexagram with its upside-down equivalent. The numbering of hexagrams in binary or decimal is a modern invention.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/confucius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/confucius.jpg" alt="Confucius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Confucius,_fresco_from_a_Western_Han_tomb_of_Dongping_County,_Shandong_province,_China_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anonymous Chinese painter of the Western Han period, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Zhou yi was written in Early Old Chinese. A copy among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> Museum's bamboo and wooden slips, discovered in 1994, shows it in use at all levels of society in its current form by 300 BCE, though small variations remained as late as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>. Another arrangement of the hexagrams, found at Mawangdui in 1973, groups them by shared upper trigram. The oldest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, found in 1987 and now in the Shanghai Library, was almost certainly in the King Wen sequence.</p><p>When the Zhou yi was canonised, it was bound to ten commentaries called the Ten Wings. They are of a much later date, written in a predecessor of Middle Chinese, and their specific origins remain a complete mystery to academics. The most important is arguably the Great Commentary (Xi ci or Dazhuan), dated to roughly 300 BCE. It describes the book as a microcosm of the universe and explains how the eight trigrams proceeded from the oneness of the universe through three bifurcations.</p><p>The Ten Wings were traditionally attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>, possibly through a misreading of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a>. Evidence suggests he did not consider the Zhou yi a classic.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhu-xi.jpg" alt="Zhu Xi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%94%AF%E4%B8%80%E5%8F%AF%E9%9D%A0%E7%9A%84%E6%9C%B1%E7%86%B9%E7%94%BB%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">向史公哲曰, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 136 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a> named the Zhou yi the first among the classics and called it the Classic of Changes, or I Ching. It joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-books-and-five-classics">Five Classics</a>, and the official edition was set in stone as one of the Xiping Stone Classics. It was not included in the burning of the Confucian classics. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Eastern Han</a> its interpreters split into two schools, one seeking symbolic and numerological parallels with nature, the other attending to the moral content. Wang Bi later discarded Han numerology, and under the Tang, Kong Yingda's Right Meaning of the Book of Changes, built on Wang Bi's commentary, became the standard edition through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>.</p><p>By the 11th century the book was read as intricate philosophy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheng-yi-philosopher">Cheng Yi</a> read it as a guide to moral perfection. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a>, in the 12th century, argued that it was primarily a work of divination usable for moral self-cultivation. In 1557 the Korean philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yi-hwang">Yi Hwang</a> produced an influential study. In Edo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> over 1,000 books on the I Ching were published by over 400 authors, and some early Edo writers ranked it the greatest of the Confucian classics.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> was especially fond of the book and ordered new interpretations.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The canonised I Ching was the standard text for over two thousand years, until alternate versions of the Zhou yi and related texts were found in the 20th century. Most early commentary, such as the image and number work of Jing Fang, Yu Fan and Xun Shuang, is no longer extant; only short fragments survive in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> text, the Zhou yi jijie. Over 150 editions of earlier Chinese commentaries were reprinted in Edo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, including several texts that had been lost in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p>Divination practice also came down in reconstructed form. The Great Commentary describes a process using a bundle of 50 yarrow stalks. Yarrow stalk divination dates to the Western Zhou, but its modern form is a reconstruction: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a>'s version, still used across the Far East, and a rival reconstruction by Gao Heng. Coin divination became widely used in the Tang dynasty and remains in use, and dice and cards have appeared more recently.</p><p>Finds of the 20th century, among them the Mawangdui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, have let scholars study how the text was used before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a>. Proponents of newly reconstructed Western Zhou readings, often very different from traditional ones, are sometimes called the modernist school.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz.jpg" alt="Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christoph_Bernhard_Francke_-_Bildnis_des_Philosophen_Leibniz_(ca._1695).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Christoph Bernhard Francke, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sinologist Michael Nylan describes the I Ching as the best-known Chinese book in the world. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> it is a foundational text for the Confucian and Daoist traditions. The Japanese word for metaphysics, keijijogaku, derives from a statement in the Great Commentary, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>'s official name, Da Yuan, was taken from the I Ching.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Leibniz</a> wrote the first commentary in 1703, arguing that the book showed the universality of binary numbers, while Hegel criticised the binary system and Chinese characters as "empty forms". The earliest complete Western translation was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> one by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> Jean-Baptiste Regis and his companions in the 1730s, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in the 1830s. James Legge and Thomas McClatchie translated it into English in the 19th century. Richard Wilhelm's 1923 German translation, put into English by Cary Baynes in 1950 with an introduction by Carl Jung, was historically the most influential.</p><p>The book had a notable impact on the 1960s counterculture and on figures such as John Cage, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-luis-borges">Jorge Luis Borges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-hesse">Hermann Hesse</a>. Pink Floyd's "Chapter 24" (1967) quotes the Wilhelm and Baynes text. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niels-bohr">Niels Bohr</a> is credited with finding inspiration in yin and yang symbolism, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-needham">Joseph Needham</a> argued that the book had impeded scientific development.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The I Ching, or Book of Changes, began as a Western Zhou divination manual of 64 hexagrams. With the Ten Wings added, it became one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-books-and-five-classics">Five Classics</a> in 136 BCE, and it has been read for centuries as divination, moral guidance and philosophy across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> and, since the 18th century, in the West.</em></p>

